From 6e6af0f3ac803d01221e91815e4dbffd406b0ceb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:49:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: take the frame rate a stream declares when its
 demuxer has none

A transport stream that is joined live is opened without
avformat_find_stream_info(), and its demuxer has no frame rate for it.
VideoPlayerVideo then configures the renderer with none and measures one
from the timestamps, which takes some 150 pictures (a ring of 120
intervals, then a second of them that agree): with "adjust display
refresh rate" on, the display changes its refresh rate a few seconds
into every such channel, after it has started at the rate of the GUI.

The decoder knows the rate that the bitstream declares (the timing of an
H.264 or HEVC VUI, an MPEG-2 sequence header), and the decoder of the
YBLOD fork took it at the first picture. A declaration is not always the
rate, though: the VUI timing is a clock tick, which is the frame rate
only when the stream says its rate is fixed (FFmpeg does not look), a
field-coded HEVC stream declares its field rate, and encoders exist that
declare half or twice the rate. So it is taken only once the timestamps
say the same: eight intervals in a row, between pictures in the order
they are shown and with their own timestamps, each within 0.5% of one
period of the declared rate (CDeclaredFrameRate). A variable rate, a
declaration that is not the rate of the pictures, a missing timestamp or
timestamps of a coarser clock (milliseconds) leave it untaken, and the
rate is measured as before; that measurement still runs after it and
corrects a rate that turns out other. videofpsdetect 0 (the rate of the
container only) leaves it untaken too, as it leaves the measurement.
Frame dropping is still allowed only once a rate has been measured.

CDVDVideoCodec::GetDeclaredFrameRate() says what the FFmpeg decoder read
(VideoPlayer never sets avctx->framerate); the other decoders declare
nothing.

Host test (declared_rate_test.cpp): 20 synthetic checks (25, 23.976
and 59.94 fps on a 90 kHz clock taken at the ninth picture; a field
rate, half and twice the rate, 0.8% off, a variable rate and a
millisecond clock never; a missing timestamp, a jump and a change of
declaration start again), and FFmpeg 9.0.2's decoders on two streams
opened without stream info, where the demuxer has no rate: a Blu-ray
transport stream declaring 23.976 has it taken at its ninth picture,
the same rate on Matroska's millisecond timestamps never. Five mutations
of the header make it fail.

Idea from the YBLOD fork, 93c37f9524; rewritten.
